reply:
The main gain comes from reordering the loops, not from adding threads. The original code streams 320 MB through memory 20 times, so it is limited by memory bandwidth. Each `y[i]` depends only on `x[i]` and its own previous value, so the work can be split into small blocks that fit in L1 cache. Each block gets all `reps` updates while it is in cache, and main memory is touched about once.

Every element still goes through exactly the same sequence of `y = a*x + y` operations, in the same order, as in the serial code. The checksum is therefore bit-identical for any thread count. There is no reduction, so the order in which blocks are processed doesn't matter.

Dynamic scheduling hands out blocks on demand. The faster P-cores take more blocks and the slower E-cores take fewer, so no thread sits idle waiting at the end.

