The main fix is load balancing. Rows near the middle of the image cost far more than rows near the edges, and 6 of the 10 cores are slower. A static split of the rows would leave threads idle while the unlucky ones finish. Instead, the image is cut into many small tiles (one row by 128 pixels), and threads take tiles from a shared queue (`schedule(dynamic,1)`). Faster cores simply take more tiles, and each tile is small, so the last one finishes quickly.

The second change helps each core run faster. The serial loop is one long chain where every step waits for the previous one. Two neighbouring pixels are now iterated together in the same loop, so the core always has two independent calculations to work on. When one pixel stops, the other continues alone from exactly where it was. Every pixel still uses exactly the same expressions in the same order, so the iteration counts and the checksum match the serial version exactly.

